.\" bmux.1 — man page for BMUX
.\"
.TH BMUX 1 "2026" "bmux 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.\"
.SH NAME
bmux \- terminal multiplexer with AI agent orchestration
.\"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmux
.RI [ SUBCOMMAND ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.\"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bmux
is a terminal multiplexer that adds first-class AI agent support to the
familiar tmux-style workflow. It manages panes, windows, and sessions while
simultaneously orchestrating Claude Code, OpenCode, Pi, Gemini, and custom
CLI agents as child processes. Agents communicate via an authenticated Unix
socket bus (HMAC-SHA256) and are sandboxed to the project directory using
Landlock (Linux) or Seatbelt (macOS).
.\"
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.\"
.SS Session Management
.TP
.B bmux new \fR[\fIname\fR]
Start a new named session (defaults to a generated name).
.TP
.B bmux attach \fR[\fIname\fR]
Reattach to an existing detached session.
.TP
.B bmux ls
List all sessions with their window count and creation time.
.TP
.B bmux kill \fR\fIname\fR
Terminate a session and all its agents.
.\"
.SS Agent Lifecycle
.TP
.B bmux agent spawn \fItype\fR \fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR
Spawn an AI agent of the given type.
Valid types: \fBclaude\fR, \fBopencode\fR, \fBpi\fR, \fBgemini\fR, \fBshell\fR, \fBcustom\fR.
.TP
.B bmux agent ls
List all agents with their status, model, and current cost.
.TP
.B bmux agent status \fIname\fR
Show detailed status for a specific agent.
.TP
.B bmux agent kill \fIname\fR
Terminate a specific agent without ending the session.
.\"
.SS Task Routing
.TP
.B bmux task send \fIagent\fR \fIprompt\fR
Send a task to the named agent. The agent must be idle or it will be queued.
.TP
.B bmux task send \-\-auto \fIprompt\fR
Let BMUX pick the agent. Idle agents are scored by capability, cost and idle time; the task is queued when every agent is busy.
.TP
.B bmux task ls
List queued and in-progress tasks with status and assigned agent.
.TP
.B bmux task cancel \fIid\fR
Cancel a queued (not yet started) task.
.\"
.SS Shared Context
.TP
.B bmux context get \fIkey\fR
Read a value from the session context store.
.TP
.B bmux context set \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Write a value to the session context store.
.TP
.B bmux context dump
Export the entire session context as a JSON file to stdout.
.\"
.SS Workflow Orchestration
.TP
.B bmux workflow run \fIfile.yaml\fR
Execute a workflow YAML. Steps are dispatched to agents in dependency order;
independent steps run in parallel.
.TP
.B bmux workflow status \fIid\fR
Display real-time status of a running workflow (step state, agent, cost).
.\"
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B bmux config show
Print the effective merged configuration.
.TP
.B bmux config reload
Hot-reload \fI~/.config/bmux/config.toml\fR without restarting.
.\"
.SS Data Management
.TP
.B bmux data purge
Delete all local BMUX data: audit logs, shared context, and session state.
Prints confirmation \fB"All BMUX local data purged"\fR on success.
.\"
.SH KEYBINDINGS
The default prefix key is
.BR Ctrl\-b ;
change it with \fBprefix_key\fR in the \fB[tui]\fR section.
All key sequences below assume the prefix has been pressed first.
Press the prefix twice to send it to the pane.
.PP
.TS
tab(|) box;
l | l.
Key | Action
_
\\ | Split pane horizontally
\- | Split pane vertically
h/j/k/l | Navigate between panes (Vim-style)
o | Focus the next pane
H/J/K/L | Resize pane
z | Zoom pane (full-screen toggle)
[ | Scroll mode (q or Esc to leave)
d | Detach from session
c | Create new window
n | Next window
p | Previous window
1\-9 | Go to window N
, | Rename current window
t | Send task to focused agent pane
T | Send task (auto-route to best agent)
r | Reload configuration
? | Show keybindings help
.TE
.\"
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is read from
.IR ~/.config/bmux/config.toml .
.PP
.nf
[tui]
prefix_key    = "ctrl-b"     # ctrl-<letter> or ctrl-space
default_shell = "/bin/zsh"   # default: $SHELL, then /bin/sh
status_bar    = true

[agents]
default_type  = "claude-code"   # used when spawn gets no type

[security]
sandbox       = "landlock"   # landlock | namespace | seatbelt | none

[audit]
enabled       = true
.fi
.\"
.SH SECRETS
API keys must be stored in
.IR ~/.config/bmux/secrets.toml
with permissions
.B 0600
(enforced at startup).
.PP
.nf
[keys]
anthropic = "sk-ant-..."
openai    = "sk-..."
.fi
.PP
The first key found among \fBanthropic\fR, \fBopenai\fR and \fBgoogle\fR is
passed to non-shell agents through an inherited pipe; the agent reads it from
the file descriptor named in \fBBMUX_SECRET_FD\fR. Keys are never placed in
environment variables and are redacted from audit log entries. Agents that do
not read \fBBMUX_SECRET_FD\fR (Claude Code, for example) keep using their own
login.
.\"
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/bmux/config.toml
Main configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/bmux/secrets.toml
API key storage (must be chmod 0600).
.TP
.I ~/.config/bmux/workflows/*.yaml
Workflow definition files.
.TP
.I <data>/bmux/audit/YYYY\-MM\-DD.jsonl
Structured audit log (one JSON event per line). \fI<data>\fR is
\fI~/.local/share\fR on Linux and \fI~/Library/Application Support\fR on macOS.
.TP
.I <data>/bmux/context/
Shared context KV store (sled).
.TP
.I /tmp/bmux\-<session>.sock
Session socket (mode 0600).
.\"
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Start a session, spawn an agent, send it a task:
.PP
.nf
bmux new my-project
bmux agent spawn claude --name dev
bmux task send dev "Refactor the auth module to use async/await"
.fi
.PP
Use a workflow to coordinate multiple agents:
.PP
.nf
bmux workflow run ~/.config/bmux/workflows/ci-pipeline.yaml
bmux workflow status <returned-id>
.fi
.PP
Export session context for debugging:
.PP
.nf
bmux context dump > context-snapshot.json
.fi
.\"
.SH SECURITY
.B bmux
applies filesystem sandboxing to each agent process:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Linux 5.13+:
Landlock ruleset restricting access to the project directory and
.IR /tmp .
.IP \(bu 2
.B Linux < 5.13:
Mount-namespace isolation (reduced protection; a warning is logged).
.IP \(bu 2
.B macOS:
Seatbelt (sandbox_init) with a generated SBPL profile.
.PP
Disable sandboxing with \fBsandbox = "none"\fR in config (not recommended;
a warning is logged at startup).
.\"
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error (see stderr).
